{"@context":{"lean":"lean:","block":"block:","imports":{"@type":"@id"}},"@graph":[{"@id":"lean:module:QOU.Archimedean.DilogPartBBranchPointLimit","@type":"LeanModule","name":"QOU.Archimedean.DilogPartBBranchPointLimit","imports":["lean:module:Init","lean:module:QOU.Archimedean.DilogPartBBranchPointSum","lean:module:QOU.MathlibExt.QuadraticIrrationalBadlyApproximable"],"declarations":12,"chunks":1},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Archimedean.wp1_tendsto_expNeg_quadratic","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Archimedean.wp1_tendsto_expNeg_quadratic","kind":"theorem","line":642,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Archimedean/DilogPartBBranchPointLimit.lean#L642-L656","doc":"**Unconditional Dilog Part B W-P1 limit for `x = e^{−β}`, `β` any quadratic\nirrational.**  If `β > 0` is irrational and satisfies an integer quadratic\n`Aβ² + Bβ + C = 0` (`A ≠ 0`), the maximal-cell scaling realizes\n`→ π²/6 − Li₂(e^{−β})` unconditionally — the general degree-2 Liouville bound\n(`QOU.MathlibExt.quadratic_irrational_fract_badlyApproximable`) supplies the\nfract-bound.  Widens `wp1_tendsto_expNegSqrt` (pure surds `√D`) to *all* real\nquadratic irrationals. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Archimedean.wp1_tendsto_expNegSqrt","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Archimedean.wp1_tendsto_expNegSqrt","kind":"theorem","line":626,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Archimedean/DilogPartBBranchPointLimit.lean#L626-L640","doc":"**Unconditional Dilog Part B W-P1 limit for `x = e^{−√D}`** — the quadratic-surd\ninstance of `wp1_tendsto_expNeg_of_fractBound`.  For any irrational `√D`,\n`sqrt_fract_badlyApproximable` supplies the fract-bound, so the limit\n`→ π²/6 − Li₂(e^{−√D})` holds unconditionally.  This is the payoff of the\nDiophantine Mathlib-gap infra: the open uniform Diophantine input is *proved* for\nthe quadratic-surd class. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Archimedean.wp1_tendsto_expNeg_of_fractBound","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Archimedean.wp1_tendsto_expNeg_of_fractBound","kind":"theorem","line":503,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Archimedean/DilogPartBBranchPointLimit.lean#L503-L624","doc":"**Unconditional Dilog Part B W-P1 limit for `x = e^{−α}`, `α` badly approximable.**\nFor any `α > 0` admitting a fractional-part badly-approximable bound\n`{jα} ≥ c/j` (`j ≥ 1`), the maximal-cell branch-point scaling `q_j = e^{1/(j+1)}`,\n`n_j = ⌊(j+1)α⌋` realizes the W-P1 limit `→ π²/6 − Li₂(e^{−α})` **unconditionally**:\nthe bound supplies the `K = 2` quadratic gap `c/e³·(q_j−1)² ≤ 1 − x q_j^{n_j}`,\ndischarging the Diophantine hypothesis of `wp1_tendsto_of_powerBound`.  This is the\nanalysis half, decoupled from the number theory: any badly-approximable `α` (the\nfract-bound is the sole input) yields the limit — quadratic surds are one instance\n(`wp1_tendsto_expNegSqrt`).  The bound alone gives `{jα} > 0` (branch below 1), so\nno separate irrationality hypothesis is needed. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Archimedean.wp1_tendsto_of_powerBound","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Archimedean.wp1_tendsto_of_powerBound","kind":"theorem","line":452,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Archimedean/DilogPartBBranchPointLimit.lean#L452-L477","doc":"**W-P1 branch-point limit for a Diophantine `x` (polynomial no-resonance).**  As\n`wp1_tendsto_of_noResonance` but with the gap bounded below by any fixed power\n`c·(q−1)^K ≤ 1−xqⁿ` — the regime a badly-approximable `x` provides (`K = 2`).  The\nupper bound forces `xqⁿ → 1⁻` (Abel); the polynomial lower bound kills the boundary\nerror.  W-P1 `→ π²/6 − Li₂(x)`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Archimedean.boundary_tendsto_zero_of_powerBound","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Archimedean.boundary_tendsto_zero_of_powerBound","kind":"theorem","line":416,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Archimedean/DilogPartBBranchPointLimit.lean#L416-L450","doc":"**Polynomial no-resonance — the badly-approximable regime.**  If the branch-point\ngap is bounded below by a fixed power of the cell width, `c·(q−1)^K ≤ 1−xqⁿ`, the\nboundary term still vanishes.  This is what a **Diophantine (badly-approximable)\n`x`** supplies: for `q_j = e^{1/j}` the maximal-cell gap is\n`≈ {j·log(1/x)}·(q_j−1)`, and bounded continued-fraction partial quotients give the\nthree-distance lower bound `{j·log(1/x)} ≥ c/j ≈ c(q_j−1)`, i.e. a *quadratic*\nbound `K = 2`.  The `K = 1` case is `boundary_tendsto_zero_of_noResonance`.  Proof\nvia `t·log t → 0`, feeding `boundary_tendsto_zero_of_rate`.  (The Diophantine fact\n\"badly-approximable ⇒ quadratic gap\" is the genuine number-theoretic input and is\nthe remaining open piece; here it is the explicit hypothesis `hlb`.) ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Archimedean.boundary_tendsto_zero_of_rate","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Archimedean.boundary_tendsto_zero_of_rate","kind":"theorem","line":392,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Archimedean/DilogPartBBranchPointLimit.lean#L392-L414","doc":"**Sharp general form — vanishing at any controllable rate.**  The boundary term\n`(q−1)·log(1−xqⁿ) → 0` whenever the branch-point gap is bounded below by a\npositive `g_j` whose own width-scaled log vanishes, `(q−1)·log(g_j) → 0`.  This is\nthe exact envelope of the no-resonance analysis: the term dies precisely when the\ngap does not shrink faster than a rate the width `q−1` can absorb.\n`boundary_tendsto_zero_of_bounded` (`g ≡ 1−ρ`), `_of_noResonance` (`g = c(q−1)`),\nand `_of_powerBound` (`g = c(q−1)^K`) are the constant / linear / polynomial\nspecial cases. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Archimedean.exists_noResonance_branchPoint_scaling","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Archimedean.exists_noResonance_branchPoint_scaling","kind":"theorem","line":254,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Archimedean/DilogPartBBranchPointLimit.lean#L254-L390","doc":"**Non-vacuity at the branch point — an explicit realizing scaling.**  There is a\nconcrete branch-point scaling `q_j = ((1 - 1/m_j)/x)^{1/m_j}`, `n_j = m_j`\n(`m_j = j + N₀`, `N₀ > 1/(1-x)`), along which the W-P1 sum converges to\n`π²/6 − Li₂(x)`.  By construction `x q_j^{n_j} = 1 - 1/m_j → 1⁻` (branch point),\nand the no-resonance bound holds with `c = x/\\log(1/x)` because\n`q_j - 1 \\le (1/x)^{1/m_j} - 1 \\le (\\log(1/x)/x)\\,(1/m_j)` (from `e^t-1 \\le t e^t`).\nThis witnesses that `wp1_tendsto_of_noResonance` fires at the branch point, not\nonly in the bulk — the dilog limit is *attained* along an explicit sequence, with\nthe open Diophantine content being only the *general-x uniform* statement. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Archimedean.wp1_tendsto_of_noResonance","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Archimedean.wp1_tendsto_of_noResonance","kind":"theorem","line":221,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Archimedean/DilogPartBBranchPointLimit.lean#L221-L252","doc":"**W-P1 branch-point limit under no resonance.**  Combining the two branch-point\ncontrols — the automatic upper bound `1 − xqⁿ ≤ (q−1)/q` (the branch-point cell\n`n = ⌊log_q(1/x)⌋`) and the no-resonance lower bound `c·(q−1) ≤ 1 − xqⁿ` — the\npositive-factor rescaled log-sum converges to `π²/6 − Li₂(x)`.  The upper bound\nforces `x qⁿ → 1⁻` (branch point, so `Li₂(xqⁿ) → π²/6` by Abel); the lower bound\nkills the boundary error (`boundary_tendsto_zero_of_noResonance`).  No opaque\nhypothesis remains — only the geometric no-resonance bound, which *is* the open\nDiophantine input, now stated as a checkable inequality rather than an assumed\nlimit. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Archimedean.boundary_tendsto_zero_of_noResonance","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Archimedean.boundary_tendsto_zero_of_noResonance","kind":"theorem","line":172,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Archimedean/DilogPartBBranchPointLimit.lean#L172-L219","doc":"**No-resonance boundary vanishing.**  The genuine branch-point regime.  If the\nscaling keeps the branch-point gap *comparable to the cell width* — a lower\nbound `c·(q−1) ≤ 1 − x qⁿ` for some fixed `c > 0` (the Faddeev / Diophantine\nno-resonance condition: the branch point does not fall super-exponentially\nclose to a lattice node `qᵏ`) — then the boundary term `(q−1)·log(1−xqⁿ) → 0`\nunconditionally as `q → 1⁺`.  This is where the general limit *fails* without a\nhypothesis (bean qou-jplv): only the upper bound `1 − xqⁿ ≤ (q−1)/q` is\nautomatic; this lemma supplies the missing lower bound and closes the term via\nthe `t·log t → 0` squeeze `(q−1)·log(c(q−1)) ≤ (q−1)·log(1−xqⁿ) ≤ 0`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Archimedean.boundary_tendsto_zero_of_bounded","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Archimedean.boundary_tendsto_zero_of_bounded","kind":"theorem","line":134,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Archimedean/DilogPartBBranchPointLimit.lean#L134-L170","doc":"**Bulk sub-case — the boundary hypothesis holds for free away from the branch\npoint.**  If the scaling stays bounded below the branch point (`x qⁿ ≤ ρ < 1`),\nthen `1 − x qⁿ ≥ 1 − ρ > 0`, so `log(1 − x qⁿ)` is bounded and the boundary\nterm `(q − 1)·log(1 − x qⁿ) → 0` unconditionally as `q → 1⁺`.  This discharges\nthe `hbdry` hypothesis of `wp1_tendsto_of_boundary` in the tame regime,\nwitnessing that the hypothesis is non-vacuous. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Archimedean.wp1_tendsto_of_boundary","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Archimedean.wp1_tendsto_of_boundary","kind":"theorem","line":90,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Archimedean/DilogPartBBranchPointLimit.lean#L90-L132","doc":"**W-P1 branch-point limit — conditional on the boundary term vanishing.**\nAlong any scaling `(q_j, n_j)` with `q_j → 1⁺`, `x q_j^{n_j} < 1`, and\n`x q_j^{n_j} → 1⁻` (branch point), the positive-factor rescaled log-sum\nconverges to `π²/6 − Li₂(x)` **provided** the single scalar boundary term\n`(q_j − 1)·log(1 − x q_j^{n_j}) → 0`.  The proof squeezes the\n`wp1_riemann_bound` error to `0`; the boundary hypothesis is the only\nnon-derived input (the open Faddeev/Diophantine limit), threaded explicitly. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Archimedean.wp1_bulkTerm_tendsto_zero","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Archimedean.wp1_bulkTerm_tendsto_zero","kind":"theorem","line":64,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Archimedean/DilogPartBBranchPointLimit.lean#L64-L88","doc":"**The always-vanishing bulk term.**  For `0 < x` and a scaling with\n`x·qⁿ < 1` (so `qⁿ < 1/x` is bounded) and `q → 1⁺`, the second error term\n`(q−1)·qⁿ·(−log(1−x))` tends to `0` — it carries no branch-point singularity,\nonly the vanishing width `q−1` times a bounded factor. ","signatureChunk":0}]}